Madhyamā.

Vaikharī.

The manifestation of speech through the powers of consciousness.

Well, here the Goddess of Speech presides over the teaching.

On the left and right are:

Gaṇapati.

And:

Guru.

The remover of obstacles and the spiritual teacher.

Then, upon the supporting lotus, the deity appropriate to the scripture being explained is worshipped.

Now, think about the symbolism.

Speech at the centre.

The guru and Gaṇapati in their appropriate places.

The presiding deity of the teaching established beneath.

And the entire act of scriptural exposition becomes a sacred rite.

Not merely an academic discussion conducted in a completely neutral setting.

The speech itself is divine.

And the teacher is about to articulate the meaning of scripture within that consecrated field.

Now, I rather love this.

Because it means that explaining philosophy isn’t simply transferring information.

Within the ritual tradition, it’s a form of worship.

Vyākhyā itself becomes yāga.

And the teacher now proceeds to explain the text.

But Abhinavagupta gives some rather demanding instructions about how they must do it.

And honestly, these are still excellent scholarly principles.

23. The proper commentator must understand the argument before refuting it
Right.

The teacher has worshipped the divine circle.

The students are attentive.

The scripture is ready.

Now comes the actual exposition.

And Abhinavagupta says the teacher should explain:

Sūtra.

Aphorisms.

Vākya.

Statements.

Paṭala.

Chapters or sections.

And:

Grantha.

The text as a whole.

But here’s the essential requirement.

Pūrvāpara-aviruddha.

Without contradiction between what comes before and what comes after.

Now, that’s an excellent principle.

Because imagine someone explains one Sanskrit verse in a particular way.

Then, two pages later, explains another verse in a way that completely contradicts their earlier interpretation.

And doesn’t even notice.

Well, that’s not terribly good scholarship, is it?

Abhinavagupta says the commentator must preserve consistency.

And he names several interpretive methods.

T antra.

The governing system.

Āvṛtti.

Repetition.

Prasaṅga.

Contextual implication or application.

Samuccaya.

Combination.

Vikalpa.

Alternative possibilities.

And other principles of scriptural reasoning.

Now, the technical details belong to the broader Indian tradition of hermeneutics.

But here’s the core instruction.

First establish the opposing position correctly.

Pūrvapakṣa.

Now, that’s absolutely magnificent.

Because it means you don’t begin by presenting a ridiculous caricature of your opponent.

You need to reconstruct the position properly.

Samyak ghaṭayitvā.

Having properly established it.

Then:

Dūṣayan.

Refute it.

And:

Sādhayan.

Establish the intended conclusion.

And finally:

Tātparya-vṛtti.

Show the purport or intended meaning.

Now, that’s brilliant.

Because the teacher is being instructed to do four things.

Understand the argument.

Explain the opposing interpretation properly.

Demonstrate why it is inadequate.

And establish the intended conclusion through the text’s actual meaning.

Now imagine someone commenting upon Abhinavagupta.

They announce:

“Right, Buddhism is wrong.”

“Why?”

“Because Abhinavagupta says so.”

“Have you understood the Buddhist position?”

“No.”

“Can you explain the relevant arguments about momentariness and self-awareness?”

“Not really.”

“Have you reconstructed the pūrvapakṣa?”

“No.”

“Then what exactly are you doing?”

Precisely.

Abhinavagupta expects the commentator to do the intellectual work.

And here’s another instruction.

Explain only up to the end of the chapter.

Paṭalāntam.

No further.

Now, apparently the teacher isn’t supposed to get so carried away that they start explaining the next seventeen chapters before anyone has had an opportunity to absorb the first one.

A rather useful warning.

At the completion of each topic, the appropriate offering and worship are performed.

And this continues until the explanation ends.

Then the sacred seat of knowledge is honoured.

Vidyāpīṭha.

The teaching seat is formally dismissed.

The ritual site is cleaned and treated according to the prescribed procedure, including its deposition in deep water.

And that concludes:

Vyākhyā-vidhi.

The rite of scriptural exposition.

Now, that might be one of my favourite parts of Chapter Twenty.

Because Abhinavagupta has just described serious philosophical argument as a sacred act.

Not an alternative to devotion.

But an expression of it.

You must understand the opposing argument.

Maintain consistency.

Explain the actual purport.

And conduct the teaching within the consecrated field of divine speech.

Now that is a proper Sanskrit lesson.

But we’ve still got two sections remaining.

And the next one is rather severe.

Because apparently failing to observe your initiated commitments can produce a hundred years as a flesh-eating spirit.

Yes.

Really.

Abhinavagupta doesn’t exactly believe in gentle reminders.

24. Samayaniṣkṛti — what happens when you break your vows?
Now we reach:

Samayaniṣkṛti.

समयनिष्कृति

The expiation or rectification of transgressed initiatory observances.

Let’s break it down.

Samaya — vow, religious commitment or prescribed observance.

Niṣkṛti — release, expiation, remedy or making good.

Now, here’s the first distinction.

For someone genuinely established in knowledge of reality:

Tattvajñāna-niṣṭha.

Abhinavagupta says the ordinary concern with expiation does not have the same application.

Prāyaścittādi na kiṃcit.

Expiation and such procedures are nothing for that person in the ultimate sense.

But here’s the important qualification.

There are others who depend upon:

Caryā.

Religious conduct.

And for those people, the prescribed observances are directly connected with their route toward liberation.

So the teacher should explain the appropriate rules.

Now, the distinction is crucial.

The realised knower isn’t spiritually constituted in the same way as a practitioner whose progress still depends upon formal religious action.

But that doesn’t mean all rules are simply meaningless for everyone.

Because the text is explicitly concerned with people whose liberation depends upon practising the prescribed discipline.

And Abhinavagupta gives a rather frightening traditional consequence for serious violations.

Someone who transgresses the vows and fails to perform expiation is said to become:

Kravyāda.

क्रव्याद

A flesh-eating being.

For:

Varṣaśatam.

One hundred years.

Now, that’s a severe traditional claim about postmortem consequences.

It is not an independently verified prediction, and it shouldn’t be used to frighten someone into remaining in a religious community.

But the doctrinal purpose is clear.

The vows are not being treated as casual suggestions.

The person who depends upon the ritual path must take the commitments seriously.

And now Abhinavagupta gives the expiatory procedure.

But there’s an especially difficult textual detail we need to acknowledge first.

25. The disturbing exception concerning the killing of a woman
The Sanskrit contains the sentence:

Strīvadhe prāyaścittaṃ nāsti.

स्त्रीवधे प्रायश्चित्तं नास्ति।

Literally:

In the killing of a woman, there is no expiation.

Now, this is an ethically serious and interpretively difficult statement.

One possible reading is that the offence is regarded as too grave to be expiated through the available ritual procedure. The bare Sanskrit does not explain that rationale, however, and the passage shouldn’t be silently rewritten as though its meaning were unambiguous.

Nor should it be presented as evidence that killing a woman is permitted or morally inconsequential.

We’re preserving the wording and acknowledging the uncertainty.

Then Abhinavagupta says that for other transgressions, the seriousness of the offence must be assessed.

Balābala.

Its relative strength or gravity.

And the appropriate repetition of the Goddess Mālinī is prescribed.

How many repetitions?

From one.

Ekavāra.

Up to:

Trilakṣa.

Three hundred thousand.

Now, that’s quite a range.

Imagine going to the teacher.

“Guruji, I’ve broken an observance.”

“Right.”

“How much japa?”

“Depends upon the gravity.”

“Could be one repetition.”

“Wonderful.”

“Or three hundred thousand.”

“WHAT?”

But the principle is that the quantity corresponds to the gravity of the transgression and the disappearance of the practitioner’s doubt.

Śaṅkā-vicyuti.

The removal or falling away of doubt.

Then comes special worship.

Viśeṣapūjā.

And again:

Cakrayāga.

The worship of the divine circle.

Because apparently no matter how far we’ve travelled through this chapter, we keep returning to the circle.

And Abhinavagupta says the cakrayāga is a concluding element in all such rites.

Now, the significant distinction is that ritual expiation is understood as a way of restoring the practitioner’s relationship with the observances.

Not a licence to harm people and then simply chant a mantra to avoid responsibility.

In actual life, wrongdoing requires appropriate accountability, repair where possible and protection of those harmed.

The historical ritual can be studied without mistaking it for an alternative to those obligations.

And with that, we reach the final part of the chapter.

Guru-pūjā.

Worship of the teacher.

And it’s a rather extraordinary conclusion.

Because we’ve just spent an entire chapter explaining what an initiated person should do with the rest of their life.

And Abhinavagupta finishes by explaining how to honour the person through whom the teaching was transmitted.

26. The day after the ritual, the guru becomes the principal deity
Right.

The ceremony has ended.

The divine circle has been worshipped.

The offerings are complete.

And the next day?

Guru-pūjā.

Now, Abhinavagupta explains something important.

During the preceding ritual, the guru was worshipped as one component of the ceremony.

Vidhyaṅga.

A limb or accessory of the rite.

But the guru was not necessarily the principal focus.

Prādhānya.

Now, on the following day, that changes.

The guru becomes the primary recipient of worship.

And Abhinavagupta says that failing to perform this distinct worship can produce:

Adhikāra-bandha.

The bondage associated with religious office.

Remember Chapter Eighteen?

The guru who possessed a particular authority but neglected its obligations could incur a corresponding fault.

Well, here we have a related conception.

The properly authorised practitioner has duties concerning the transmission and honouring of spiritual knowledge.

And the guru’s separate worship is one of them.

Now, what’s the procedure?

First, prepare:

Svastika-maṇḍala.

A sacred diagram of the svastika type.

Then establish:

Sauvarṇa-pīṭha.

A golden seat.

Now, we should understand svastika here in its ancient Indian religious context, as an auspicious geometrical motif. Its historical use in this tantric setting is distinct from the modern political appropriation of that symbol.

Then, within the seat, worship:

Samasta-adhvan.

The entire cosmic path.

All of it.

The worlds.

The tattvas.

The kalās.

The mantras.

The phonemes.

The padas.

The entire manifestation.

And after the seat has been consecrated through the worship of the cosmic pathways, the guru is established upon it.

Now think about that.

The guru is seated upon the entire universe.

That’s the ritual image.

The cosmic pathways become the consecrated throne.

And the teacher occupies the seat as the embodiment of the knowledge through which Śiva is recognised.

Now, that’s magnificent.

Because the guru isn’t merely being honoured for being a nice person.

The ritual expresses the teacher’s relationship with the supreme conscious reality.

And then come the offerings.

Tarpaṇa.

Ritual satisfaction or libation.

Bhojana.

Food.

Dakṣiṇā.

The traditional gift or honorarium.

And finally:

Ātman.

One’s own Self.

Now, that’s an extraordinary offering.

The practitioner offers themselves.

Not merely a flower.

Not merely some food.

Not merely money.

But the Self.

Now, within the traditional ritual, this expresses complete dedication to the guru.

It doesn’t grant an actual person unlimited authority over another’s body, finances or autonomy.

But its symbolic force is powerful.

The whole religious life is offered through the relationship with the teacher.

Then the disciple requests:

Naivedya-ucchiṣṭa.

The remains of the food that has been offered.

Now, this is a well-known form of religious reverence in several Indian traditions.

Receiving and consuming the guru’s remnants expresses the disciple’s participation in the sacred offering.

And Abhinavagupta says the disciple should bow respectfully.

Vanditvā.

Then consume the received portion.

Prāśya.

And finally perform:

Cakrapūjā.

Worship of the divine circle.

Now, notice how the chapter ends.

We began with the initiated disciple.

And the instruction:

This is how you live for the rest of your life.

We’ve discussed daily worship.

Mantra.

The Heart.

The altar as a mirror.

The body as a temple.

The festival calendar.

The sacred circle.

The pavitraka.

The proper explanation of scripture.

The expiation of religious transgressions.

And finally the worship of the guru.

And the concluding ceremony returns to the same principle we’ve been investigating throughout the entire text.

The guru, disciple and cosmic manifestation are not ultimately independent of the supreme consciousness.

Yet the differentiated relationships within the religious order remain meaningful.

The teacher transmits.

The disciple receives.

The ritual is performed.

The scriptures are explained.

The obligations are observed.

And through all of it, the underlying aim is:

Tanmayībhāva.

Identity with Śiva.

Now, that completes the entire prose of Chapter Twenty.

The GRETIL Sanskrit transcription ends here, at 20.65, with the words:

Iti gurupūjāvidhiḥ.

Thus concludes the procedure of guru worship.

But the Devanāgarī edition preserves one final stanza.

And it is absolutely extraordinary.

Because after all this detailed instruction concerning lifelong religious discipline, Abhinavagupta makes a startling claim.

You can attain liberation through proper conduct alone, even without independently achieving knowledge and yoga.

Now, that’s a major statement.

Let’s read the verse.

27. The concluding verse — liberation through conduct alone
ATTESTED CLOSING VERSE — CHAPTER TWENTY

नित्यं नैमित्तिकं कर्म कुर्वञ्शाठ्यविवर्जितः।विनापि ज्ञानयोगाभ्यां चर्यामात्रेण मुच्यते॥
IAST TRANSLITERATION

Nityaṃ naimittikaṃ karma kurvañ śāṭhya-vivarjitaḥ.

Vināpi jñāna-yogābhyāṃ caryā-mātreṇa mucyate.

This stanza is preserved at the end of the Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism edition⁠Attachment.png. It is absent from GRETIL’s 1918-edition transcription, which ends after the guru-pūjā procedure. The two devotional verses earlier in this chapter are present in both witnesses. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism +1

Now.

Let’s unpack this properly.

Nityaṃ naimittikaṃ karma.

Regular and occasional religious actions.

Nitya — obligatory, regularly prescribed.

Naimittika — performed on particular occasions.

Karma — action, here ritual activity.

Then:

Kurvan.

Performing.

And:

Śāṭhya-vivarjitaḥ.

Free from deceit or dishonesty.

Let’s break that down.

Śāṭhya — fraudulence, hypocrisy, deceit.

Vivarjita — free from, devoid of.

So:

Someone who performs the regular and occasional observances without deceit.

Now comes the astonishing part.

Vinā api jñāna-yogābhyām.

Even without knowledge and yoga.

Vinā — without.

Jñāna — knowledge.

Yoga — yogic practice or absorption.

Then:

Caryā-mātreṇa.

Through conduct alone.

And finally:

Mucyate.

Is liberated.

So the verse means:

Someone who sincerely performs the obligatory and occasional observances, without deceit, can attain liberation through conduct alone, even without separately attaining knowledge and yoga.

Now.

That’s a remarkable ending to Chapter Twenty.

Because consider where the Tantrasāra began.

Chapter One.

Jñānaṃ mokṣakāraṇam.

Knowledge is the cause of liberation.

We spent all that time examining the philosophical nature of liberating knowledge.

And here Abhinavagupta appears to say that someone may be liberated through ritual conduct alone.

Isn’t that a contradiction?

Well.

Let’s think carefully.

Because the chapter is not describing any random person performing arbitrary rituals.

It’s discussing someone who has already entered the appropriate initiatory system.

A recipient of the relevant dīkṣā.

Someone whose subsequent life is governed by the discipline communicated by the guru.

And whose actions are directed toward:

Tanmayībhāva.

Identity with the deity.

So ritual conduct here isn’t simply external mechanical activity detached from consciousness.

It’s a prescribed process within a system that attributes liberating efficacy to divine grace and initiation.

Now, the person’s understanding may not independently reach the philosophical or yogic heights described earlier in the text.

But the ritual path is nevertheless understood as capable of leading to liberation.

And that’s why the verse is so important.

Because Abhinavagupta isn’t presenting only one kind of practitioner.

Some may recognise directly.

Some through contemplation.

Some through mantra.

Some through yoga.

And others through the faithful performance of the initiated discipline.

Different means.

Different capacities.

One ultimate reality.

And the distinctive requirement of this closing verse is:

Śāṭhya-vivarjitaḥ.

Without deceit.

Not merely performing the ritual outwardly while deliberately corrupting its purpose.

Not using religious practice as a means of exploitation.

Not simply going through the motions to acquire status.

But sincerely fulfilling the prescribed observances.

And within the traditional theology, such conduct can become an effective path of liberation.

Now, that’s a rather extraordinary conclusion.

Because the disciple we met at the beginning of the chapter wasn’t necessarily a great philosopher.

Perhaps they couldn’t explain the Pratyabhijñā arguments.

Perhaps they didn’t understand the deepest points of Sanskrit grammar.

Perhaps they couldn’t distinguish every one of the thirty-six tattvas.

But they received initiation.

They followed the guru’s instructions.

They practised.

They worshipped.

They maintained the discipline.

And Abhinavagupta’s concluding verse says that even such a person can attain liberation.

Now, that is quite significant.

The highest reality is not reserved exclusively for people who can write magnificent philosophical arguments.

And that brings us to the end.

End of Chapter Twenty — Śeṣavartanaprakāśanam
Now consider the extraordinary journey of this chapter.

It began with a very practical question.

What should an initiated person do for the remainder of their life?

And Abhinavagupta answered through three broad categories of religious action.

Nitya.

Naimittika.

Kāmya.

Then he turned toward the daily practice.

The oral transmission of the mantra.

Its potency.

Its essential nature as the pulsation of consciousness.

The practice at the junctions of the day.

The gradual establishment of identity with the divine.

And then the altar became a mirror.

The deity became your own reflected nature.

The body became a sacred dwelling.

The Heart became the priceless offering vessel.

And the flowers became manifestations of your own essential being.

Then we discovered that the entire ritual process has one supreme purpose.

Parameśvarābhedābhimāna.

The recognition of non-difference from Parameśvara.

And we turned toward the calendar.

The lunar dates.

The festivals.

The planetary conjunctions.

The sacred circle.

The human, Śakti and Śiva united in the ritual assembly.

Then the pavitraka.

A ceremonial thread containing the entire hierarchy of cosmic manifestation.

Followed by the sacred exposition of scripture.

And the demand that a proper teacher reconstruct the opposing argument, explain it accurately and establish the intended conclusion without contradiction.

Then the expiation of broken observances.

And finally the worship of the guru.

The entire cosmic path becomes the teacher’s seat.

And the disciple offers not merely food and flowers, but their own Self.

Now, here’s the important thing.

The chapter doesn’t abandon the metaphysics we’ve spent nineteen chapters studying.

It puts that metaphysics into daily life.

The same consciousness.

The same Śakti.

The same freedom.

The same relationship between the limited subject and the supreme Self.

But now everything becomes an opportunity for recognition.

Eating.

Worshipping.

Studying.

Singing.

Receiving a scripture.

Seeing the guru.

Celebrating a festival.

Explaining philosophy.

Even the apparently mundane business of handling a ritual offering.

All can become expressions of the divine reality.

And the culmination is not merely accumulating an ever-growing collection of ceremonies.

It’s establishing:

Tanmayībhāva.

Identity with Śiva.

So imagine the disciple asking:

“Guruji, what should I do now that I’ve been initiated?”

And Abhinavagupta replies:

“Well, mate.”

“Worship every day.”

“Remember the mantra.”

“Practise at the sacred junctions.”

“See the deity as your own essential nature.”

“Celebrate the appropriate festivals.”

“Honour the guru.”

“Understand the scriptures.”

“Perform the relevant observances.”

“And through all of it, recognise that nothing is ultimately separate from Parameśvara.”

“Right.”

“For how long?”

“The rest of your bloody life.”

And here’s the final joke.

You thought initiation would end your spiritual practice.

But Abhinavagupta has explained that your spiritual practice can become the very manner in which you live.

The whole of life becomes worship.

Not because everything you do is automatically wise or liberating.

But because the appropriate recognition can transform your relationship to everything you do.

And in the highest condition?

You’re no longer worshipping Śiva as a foreign deity.

The worshipper, the worship and the worshipped are apprehended within one unrestricted consciousness.

The whole bloody business is Śiva.

Absolutely magnificent.

