Right then.

Chapter Twenty-Two.

And, mate, what a chapter.

We have spent twenty-one chapters discussing consciousness.

Cit.

Its absolute freedom.

Svātantrya.

The emergence of the universe through Śakti.

The contraction of that universe into the limited individual.

And the various methods through which the individual recognises that the whole thing was Śiva from the beginning.

We’ve studied the scriptures.

Investigated their authority.

Examined the philosophical hierarchy of religious traditions.

And in the last chapter, Abhinavagupta finally told us to adopt the revelation whose fruit is supreme.

Anuttara.

The unsurpassable.

Very good.

But now he’s going to do something rather outrageous.

Because after all that elegant metaphysics, he returns to an esoteric form of worship.

Kulayāga.

कुलयाग

The Kaula sacrifice.

And this isn’t merely another ceremony involving flowers, incense and somebody solemnly ringing a bell.

Oh no.

We’re going to discuss six increasingly interior forms of worship.

The external altar.

Śakti.

The practitioner’s own body.

The union of Śiva and Śakti.

Vital energy.

And finally consciousness itself.

And the fourth of these?

Yāmalayāga.

The worship of the divine pair.

Which means Abhinavagupta is about to discuss sexuality as part of the cosmic manifestation of consciousness.

Now, here’s the important thing.

We’re not going to pretend that the chapter is merely a metaphor for thinking nice thoughts.

It describes a historical tantric ritual tradition in which embodied sexuality, sensory enjoyment and consecrated substances have real ceremonial significance.

But neither are we going to mistake it for:

“Have a drink, get laid, and congratulations, you’ve achieved enlightenment.”

Absolutely bloody not.

Because Abhinavagupta’s entire argument concerns the transformation of experience through:

Vimarśa.

Consciousness’s reflexive apprehension of itself.

