The body isn’t merely a container for an individual consciousness.

It becomes an embodiment of the divine circle.

Deha-cakra.

The body’s circle of powers.

But now we reach the fourth sacrifice.

And this is where Abhinavagupta suddenly abandons ordinary prose.

For an astonishing stretch, the chapter becomes almost entirely verse.

And the subject?

Yāmala.

The divine pair.

Now buckle up.

Because we’re about to investigate Śiva, Śakti, sexual union, the secret of visarga, the emergence of sound, and the nature of liberation while still alive.

And the whole thing culminates in one of the most beautiful statements in the entire Tantrasāra.

The supreme Kaula is neither the peaceful state nor the manifested state. It is the reality from which both arise.

Absolutely bloody magnificent.

12. Yāmalayāga — the divine pair
Right.

Now comes:

यामल — Yāmala.

A pair.

A couple.

A conjunction of two.

And here, of course, we’re discussing:

Śakti and Śaktimat.

Power and its possessor.

Now, listen carefully.

The first verse of this section begins:

ATTESTED VERSE — 22.17

शक्तेर्लक्षणमेतत् तद्वदभेदस्ततोऽनपेक्ष्य वयः।जात्यादींश्चासङ्गाल्लोकेतरयुगलजं हि तादात्म्यम्॥
Śakter lakṣaṇam etat tadvadabhedas tato’napekṣya vayaḥ.

Jātyādīṃś cāsaṅgāl loketarayugalajaṃ hi tādātmyam.

Now, this is extraordinary.

Śakter lakṣaṇam etat.

This is the defining characteristic of Śakti.

What is it?

Tadvat-abheda.

Non-difference from the possessor of the power.

Now that is the foundation.

Not simply that the Goddess is very powerful.

Not that she’s an independent supernatural being who happens to collaborate with Śiva.

But that she’s inseparable from the consciousness whose power she is.

And the verse goes on to invoke a union beyond ordinary classifications of age, birth-status and the like.

Vayaḥ.

Age.

Jāti.

Birth or social category.

Now, this is a historically significant Kaula challenge to conventional social distinctions. It is not a licence to disregard consent, exploit unequal relationships or involve people incapable of consenting. The ritual’s metaphysical claim and the ethical requirements for real human relationships must be distinguished.

What is the result of the union as the text understands it?

Tādātmya.

Identity of nature.

Now, that’s a word we’ve encountered repeatedly.

Tad — that.

Ātmya — having that as one’s nature.

And here the divine pair is understood through a union in which separateness is overcome.

But now Abhinavagupta makes a more intricate distinction.

The power can be described in a threefold way according to:

Kārya.

Effect.

Hetu.

Cause.

Sahottha.

That which arises together.

Now, this is an interesting triad.

Because it refuses to reduce the relation to a simple linear model.

As though Śiva were first sitting alone doing absolutely nothing.

Then suddenly decided to manufacture Śakti.

And thereafter she began manufacturing the universe.

No.

The relation of consciousness and power cannot ultimately be understood as a relation between two independently existing objects.

So the text explores causal, resultant and co-arising forms of the relation.

And then it returns to worship.

Abhyarcya.

To be honoured.

Tarpya.

To be ritually satisfied.

Why?

Ānandāntikatva.

Because of proximity to bliss.

Now, that’s rather beautiful.

The divine power is approached through her relationship with the fullness of conscious bliss.

But the ritual has a sequence.

First the principal circle.

Then the subsidiary circles.

And the distinction between external and internal worship.

Here we encounter:

Bahiḥ.

Externally.

And:

Antaḥ.

Internally.

The outward worship employs flowers and associated offerings.

The inward dimension is related to fragrance, food, wine and the experienced powers of the ritual.

Now, this is clearly an embodied Kaula context.

But the philosophical structure is the same as before.

The outer activity is not considered complete merely because the material substances have been presented.

Its inner significance concerns the awakening and expansion of divine self-awareness.

And then comes a beautiful phrase.

Ānanda-sandoha.

A mass or abundance of bliss.

Through the unfolding of this bliss, the subsidiary circles are brought into identity with the principal circle.

And then:

Abhilīyate.

They dissolve.

Now, what exactly is dissolving?

Not necessarily the participants physically disappearing.

The apparent separateness of the subordinate divine circles is being overcome in the contemplation.

The many return to the one.

And Abhinavagupta describes this in another attested verse.

13. The subsidiary goddesses return to the central circle of consciousness
ATTESTED VERSE — 22.21

निजनिजभोगाभोगप्रविकासमयस्वरूपपरिमर्शे।क्रमशोऽनुचक्रदेव्यः संविच्चक्रं हि मध्यमं यान्ति॥
Nijanijabhogābhogapravikāsamayasvarūpaparimarśe.

Kramaśo’nucakradevyaḥ saṃviccakraṃ hi madhyamaṃ yānti.

Now, let’s unpack the key words.

Nija-nija.

Each one’s own.

Bhoga-ābhoga.

Enjoyment and the field or expansion of enjoyment.

Pravikāsa.

Unfolding, blossoming, expansion.

Svarūpa-parimarśa.

Reflexive apprehension of one’s own essential nature.

And then:

Anucakra-devyaḥ.

The goddesses of the subsidiary circles.

Where do they go?

Saṃvic-cakraṃ madhyamam.

The central circle of consciousness.

Now, that is absolutely magnificent.

Because the different powers of experience aren’t merely suppressed.

The various pleasures and manifestations become occasions for the expansion of self-apprehension.

And through that expansion, the subsidiary powers are gathered into the central awareness.

Now imagine a collection of little flames.

Each apparently separate.

Each possessing its own particular brilliance.

But all expressions of one fire.

And the contemplative process brings their apparent separation back into the awareness of that one luminosity.

That’s an analogy, of course.

Abhinavagupta’s own imagery here is the divine circle.

But the principle is similar.

The plurality of powers returns to the conscious unity in which they arise.

Now comes the next stage.

The Śakti and Śaktimat become mutually oriented toward one another.

Anyonya-samunmukha.

Facing each other.

Turned toward one another.

And their power becomes filled with the rays of the subordinate deities.

Marīci-gaṇa.

A collection of rays.

Now think about this imagery.

The central pair isn’t isolated from the rest of the divine manifestation.

The subsidiary powers are like rays returning toward their source.

And the pair is enriched through that complete gathering.

Then comes:

Ūrdhva-dhāma.

The upper abode.

And:

Saṃkṣobha.

A stirring or agitation.

Now, the ritual context includes embodied union and the heightened experience associated with it.

But the text is interested in what happens to the awareness of differentiation.

The subordinate circles are stirred.

And then:

Tanmayāni na pṛthak.

They become of that nature, not separate.

Now, that’s the crucial phrase.

Not separate.

The many powers are no longer apprehended as independent.

And then Abhinavagupta gives us what may be the single most important verse in this entire section.

14. When every conversation about separation has ceased
ATTESTED VERSE — 22.24

इत्थं यामलमेतद्गलितभिदासङ्कथं यदैव तदा।क्रमतारतम्ययोगात्सैव हि संविद्विसर्गसङ्घट्टः॥
Itthaṃ yāmalam etad galitabhidāsaṃkathaṃ yadaiva tadā.

Kramatāratamyayogāt saiva hi saṃvidvisargasaṃghaṭṭaḥ.

Now listen carefully.

Galita-bhidā-saṃkatha.

What an extraordinary expression.

Galita — fallen away, dissolved, melted.

Bhidā — division, difference.

Saṃkathā — discourse, talk, discussion.

So:

The very conversation about difference has melted away.

Now, that’s gorgeous.

Not merely the philosophical assertion:

“Śiva and Śakti are one.”

But a condition in which the discourse of separateness no longer applies.

And Abhinavagupta connects this with:

Saṃvid-visarga-saṅghaṭṭa.

Let’s unpack it.

Saṃvid — consciousness.

Visarga — emission, creative release.

Saṅghaṭṭa — impact, conjunction, coming together.

So:

The conjunction of consciousness and its creative emission.

Now, that is an extraordinary description of the divine pair.

Because the Kaula rite isn’t merely interpreted through physical union.

It’s understood through the fundamental relationship between consciousness and its power to manifest.

And remember:

Śakti isn’t a foreign thing.

She is the freedom of Śiva.

Svātantrya.

So the conjunction doesn’t produce a new absolute reality that didn’t exist before.

It’s the conscious apprehension of the inseparability of the two.

And here’s the really magnificent thing.

Abhinavagupta now asks:

What is the supreme Kaula reality that underlies this entire process?

And he answers in a verse that is absolutely worth learning by heart.

15. The supreme Kaula is neither the peaceful nor the manifest
ATTESTED VERSE — 22.25

तद्ध्रुवधामानुत्तरमुभयात्मकजगदुदारमानन्दम्।नो शान्तं नाप्युदितं शान्तोदितसूतिकारणं परं कौलम्॥
Taddhruvadhāmānuttaram ubhayātmakajagadudāram ānandam.

No śāntaṃ nāpy uditaṃ śāntoditasūtikāraṇaṃ paraṃ kaulam.

Right.

This is magnificent.

Let’s unpack it properly.

Dhruva-dhāma.

The stable, immutable abode.

Dhruva — fixed, unwavering.

Dhāman — abode, luminous domain.

Then:

Anuttara.

The unsurpassable.

Nothing higher.

Then:

Ubhayātmaka-jagad-udāra-ānanda.

A vast, generous bliss embracing the universe in its twofold character.

And then comes the really important distinction.

No śāntam.

Not merely the peaceful state.

Na api uditam.

Nor merely the arisen or manifest state.

What, then?

Śānta-udita-sūti-kāraṇam.

The cause from which both the peaceful and manifested states arise.

Now, break that down.

Śānta — peaceful, quiescent, at rest.

Udita — arisen, manifested, emerged.

Sūti — birth, production.

Kāraṇa — cause.

So:

The source of both repose and manifestation.

And what is that source?

Paraṃ kaulam.

The supreme Kaula reality.

Now, this is one of the most important philosophical points in the entire Tantrasāra.

Because you might imagine enlightenment means entering a completely peaceful condition where nothing happens.

No thoughts.

No sensations.

No desires.

No perceptions.

Just absolute stillness.

And Abhinavagupta says:

That’s not the whole thing.

Because what about manifestation?

What about the freedom through which awareness displays a universe?

What about the capacity for activity?

Experience?

Creation?

And the divine unfolding of Śakti?

If you identify the supreme reality only with the absence of manifestation, you’ve left out its power to manifest.

But now suppose you go to the other extreme.

You identify the supreme only with the blissful energy of manifested experience.

The heightened sensation.

The ecstatic movement.

The outward surge.

Again, Abhinavagupta says:

That’s not the whole thing either.

Because the supreme isn’t exhausted by the appearing state.

It’s the source of both.

The peaceful and the arisen.

Śānta and udita.

Now that is absolutely crucial.

Anuttara isn’t merely the absence of experience. Nor is it merely the intensity of experience.

It is the unrestricted conscious reality through which both repose and manifestation become possible.

And this is why the Kaula rite is philosophically much more subtle than ordinary hedonism.

It’s not simply celebrating pleasure.

It’s asking what the ultimate nature of experience is.

What is the consciousness through which pleasure appears?

Through which its cessation appears?

Through which desire arises?

Through which desire subsides?

And through which both states can be apprehended as manifestations of one freedom?

That is the real subject.

Paraṃ kaulam.

The supreme Kaula.

Now, remember Chapter Two?

The condition beyond methods.

Anupāya.

And Chapter Four?

The relationship between manifestation, recognition and the Kālīs.

All those teachings are now being drawn into one extraordinary account of the supreme.

The highest reality isn’t a particular state among other states.

It’s the reality that makes every state possible.

And Abhinavagupta immediately says what the practitioner seeking freedom should do.

Ātmasāt-kuryāt.

Make that consciousness one’s own.

Not acquire it as a foreign possession.

Recognise it as one’s own essential nature.

And that leads us to the next astonishing feature.

Śakti and Śaktimat each contain both aspects.

16. Śakti and Śiva — not two halves of an incomplete God
Now, here’s a common misunderstanding.

People say:

“Śiva is stillness.”

“Śakti is activity.”

“Śiva is consciousness.”

“Śakti is energy.”

And then imagine the two as though they were completely separate halves of a machine.

As if Śiva were a motionless bloke sitting on the sofa while Śakti did absolutely everything.

Not quite.

Abhinavagupta says:

Śānta-udita-ātmaka-dvayam.

The twofold condition of quiescence and manifestation.

And that condition arises in both:

Śakti.

And:

Śaktimat.

Why?

Because they mutually enter one another.

Sva-ātma-anyonya-āveśa.

Mutual immersion within their own nature.

And both possess the nature of both aspects.

Dvayātmatva.

The condition of being twofold.

Now, that is very important.

The distinction between Śiva and Śakti isn’t ultimately a division between two independently existing realities.

Their difference expresses a functional and contemplative polarity within one consciousness.

The peaceful aspect.

The manifest aspect.

The possessor of power.

The power.

But each is inseparable from the other.

And yet Abhinavagupta makes one further distinction.

It is Śakti who nourishes the arisen manifestation.

Śaktiḥ … sṛṣṭiṃ puṣṇāti.

Śakti sustains creation.

Now, don’t lose the subtlety.

The distinction is real within the dynamic manifestation.

But it doesn’t imply an ultimate separation.

Śakti is the manifesting power of Śiva, not a second absolute beside him.

And then comes an astonishing account of transmission.

The meaning of Kula is embodied in the teacher.

It is transmitted to Śakti.

And through her, communicated to others.

Now, this is an important part of the historical Kaula conception of transmission.

The female participant isn’t simply an incidental accessory.

She has a mediating role in the manifestation and communication of the divine power.

And the ritual is understood through an interplay of teacher, Śakti and participants.

But once again, that religious symbolism doesn’t override the ethical independence of real people.

Now, Abhinavagupta returns to the core distinction.

The peaceful and manifest conditions.

The practitioner who apprehends the prior reality of emission within both becomes established in:

Anavacchinna-pada.

The unrestricted state.

And this leads to something rather striking.

The text speaks of a transmitted emanation being received and outwardly shared within the ritual circle.

Yes, this is a historical Kaula context involving bodily and sexual ritual substances.

We’re not going to pretend otherwise.

But the governing purpose remains the satisfaction of:

Devī-cakra.

The circle of goddesses.

And Abhinavagupta describes the fruits as:

Siddhi.

Attainment.

Jñāna.

Knowledge.

Apavarga.

Liberation.

Now, that’s a very important triad.

Because the result isn’t limited to sensual pleasure.

The rite is being related to the larger tantric system of accomplishment, knowledge and liberation.

But here’s something even more interesting.

The text distinguishes the cultivation of the peaceful state from the outward manifestation of the divine powers.

And from this, Abhinavagupta develops a doctrine of:

Threefold visarga.

Three modes of emission.

Now, you might think you’ve heard enough about emission for one afternoon.

But this is the key to understanding what he says about mantra.

And we’re about to reach one of the most beautiful ideas in the chapter.

The power of a mantra is related to the living way consciousness manifests through it.

17. The threefold visarga — how the universe comes spilling out of consciousness
Right.

Now comes the next movement of the argument.

Abhinavagupta has distinguished the peaceful and manifested conditions.

Śānta.

Udita.

And established that supreme Kaula consciousness is the source of both.

But within the ritual, different modes of emission are experienced and contemplated.

And now he describes the unfolding of the divine circle through an extraordinary interplay of fullness, longing and the relish of outward manifestation.

One phrase is particularly interesting:

Raṇaraṇaka-rasa.

रणरणकरस

The flavour of longing, yearning or restless desire.

Now, that’s a beautiful expression.

Because desire isn’t always experienced simply as the possession of something enjoyable.

Sometimes it’s the sense that something hasn’t yet been fulfilled.

A movement toward an object.

An anticipation.

A longing.

And Abhinavagupta connects these movements with the emerging manifestations of consciousness.

The fullness of the inner divine circles unfolds.

Then the outward manifestation is relished.

Then the differentiated powers return toward their source.

And in that movement, the threefold character of visarga becomes intelligible.

Now, here’s what matters.

The supreme consciousness isn’t restricted to the experience of fullness before manifestation.

Nor does it cease to be itself once the outward world appears.

The longing.

The manifestation.

The return.

All occur through its own power.

And this is where the Kaula ritual becomes a particularly vivid expression of the whole Trika cosmology.

Remember the five divine acts?

Sṛṣṭi.

Manifestation.

Sthiti.

Maintenance.

Saṃhāra.

Withdrawal.

Tirobhāva.

Concealment.

Anugraha.

Grace.

Well, now imagine the activity of manifestation and return being apprehended through the living processes of embodied experience.

Not as foreign phenomena.

But as expressions of the power of consciousness.

And Abhinavagupta gives this whole process a technical consequence.

Manuvīrya.

The potency of mantra.

18. The secret of mantra potency — consciousness gives the syllable its power
ATTESTED VERSE — 22.33

एतद्विसर्गधामनि परिमर्शनतस्त्रिधैव मनुवीर्यम्।तत्तत्संविद्गर्भे मन्त्रस्तत्तत्फलं सूते॥
Etadvisargadhāmani parimarśanatas tridhaiva manuvīryam.

Tattatsaṃvidgarbhe mantras tattatphalaṃ sūte.

Now, listen carefully.

Etad-visarga-dhāmani.

Within this domain of creative emission.

Parimarśanataḥ.

Through reflexive apprehension.

Tridhā eva manuvīryam.

Mantra potency is threefold.

And then comes the remarkable second line.

Tattat-saṃvid-garbhe.

Within the womb of the corresponding conscious awareness.

Saṃvid — consciousness.

Garbha — womb, interior, generative source.

And:

Mantras tattatphalaṃ sūte.

The mantra gives birth to the corresponding fruit.

Now, sūte is a beautiful verb.

It means to generate, produce or give birth.

So the mantra’s result is described through the imagery of birth.

But where does that productive power arise?

Within consciousness.

Not as an independently existing mechanical force belonging to the syllable.

And the result depends upon the particular form of conscious apprehension.

Now, remember Chapter Fourteen?

We discussed:

Vāsanā.

The contemplative intention or orientation through which mantras produce different results.

And now we’ve arrived at a deeper account.

The mantra possesses potency through its relationship to consciousness’s own power of manifestation.

So imagine someone holding a mantra like a little magical device.

“I’ve got the syllable.”

“Excellent.”

“I’ve pronounced it correctly.”

“Very good.”

“So where’s my siddhi?”

Ah.

You’ve missed something rather important.

The living power of consciousness through which the mantra is understood and manifested.

The text is not saying Sanskrit phonemes function as independently powerful switches that mechanically produce supernatural events.

Within Trika’s theological account, their efficacy depends upon the divine power of awareness.

And the Kaula rite is concerned with recognising that power at its most intimate.

Now, here’s where the chapter introduces another extraordinary symbolic arrangement.

Lotuses.

A pair of lotuses.

A stalk.

A triangle.

The Sun.

The Moon.

Fire.

And the emergence of a sprout.

Apparently we’ve moved from metaphysics to a rather elaborate botanical astronomy lesson.

But the symbolism is magnificent.

19. The two lotuses, Sun, Moon and fire — the birth of manifestation
The verses beginning at 22.34 describe a lotus situated within a three-cornered arrangement.

Koṇatraya.

The threefold enclosure.

And the lotus possesses:

Ṣoḍaśadala.

Sixteen petals.

Now, remember Chapter Three?

The sixteen vowels.

The great phonemic powers associated with the manifestation of Śakti.

A sixteen-petalled lotus is therefore particularly suggestive within this system.

The text doesn’t explicitly identify every petal with a Sanskrit vowel in this passage, so we needn’t invent a complete correspondence.

But the broader association is worth recognising.

Now there are two lotuses joined through a central stalk.

Nāla.

And the text describes their conjunction.

Ghaṭṭana.

Contact.

Coming together.

And the emergence of fire.

Agni.

Then comes a remarkable image.

The rays of the Moon and the Sun meet.

Śaśadhara.

The Moon-bearer.

The Moon.

Dinakara.

The maker of day.

The Sun.

And:

Citragu.

Fire, within this symbolic triad.

Now we’re discussing a conjunction of three cosmic powers.

Moon.

Sun.

Fire.

And from the meeting of these powers, the text describes the arising of a sprout.

Aṅkura-sṛṣṭi.

अङ्कुरसृष्टि

The manifestation of a sprout.

Now, a sprout is a magnificent symbol.

Because it represents something previously latent coming into manifestation.

The seed possessed a potential.

The appropriate conditions came together.

And now something arises.

Unmeṣa.

Opening forth.

And within this Kaula imagery, the conjunction of the solar, lunar and fiery powers expresses the generative unfolding of divine manifestation.

The historical imagery also carries bodily and sexual symbolism, including generative fluids. That is part of the ritual’s esoteric vocabulary, not merely an abstract astronomical allegory.

But what’s the philosophical point?

Manifestation is a creative movement of consciousness, not an independently existing process outside it.

And Abhinavagupta says that through the relevant mudrā associated with this union, the practitioner brings the sequences of creation and related activities into the interior field of awareness.

And the result is:

Turya.

The fourth state.

Now, that is a rather extraordinary conclusion.

Because the aim isn’t merely a bodily experience.

It is the apprehension of the divine consciousness underlying the ordinary differentiated states.

And from here, Abhinavagupta introduces:

Khecarīmudrā.

The seal of Khecarī.

And this is where the chapter becomes almost phenomenological.

Because he’s about to discuss sound.

Laughter.

Movement.

Pleasure.

And the extraordinary way these experiences can become expressions of conscious self-awareness.

20. Khecarī — laughter, enjoyment and the living movement of awareness
Now, remember:

Khecarī.

खेचरी

* Kha* — space, the open expanse.

Carī — she who moves.

So:

She who moves through the expanse.

Within the Trika context, this isn’t simply physical movement through the sky.

It’s associated with the unrestricted activity of consciousness.

And the next verse gives us a surprisingly ordinary list of human experiences.

Pāna.

Drinking.

Upabhoga.

Enjoyment.

Līlā.

Play.

Hāsa.

Laughter.

Now, isn’t that wonderful?

After all the dense Sanskrit metaphysics, Abhinavagupta is suddenly discussing people laughing.

And the crucial word is:

Vimarśamaya.

Consisting of self-apprehending awareness.

The practitioner is to recognise the divine power through the living activity of experience.

Now, here’s the difference.

Ordinarily, you laugh.

And you become absorbed in whatever made you laugh.

Perhaps somebody said something ridiculous.

Perhaps a situation caught you off guard.

And the experience unfolds.

But within the Kaula contemplation, the practitioner recognises the consciousness through which the laughter arises.

Not as a detached little spectator thinking:

“Interesting, I am currently laughing.”

But as the self-aware luminosity that is inseparable from the experience.

The laughing itself can become an expression of vimarśa.

And Abhinavagupta extends this beyond laughter.

To pleasure.

Play.

Drinking.

And the entire range of embodied manifestation.

Now, that’s an important distinction.

He’s not saying that any behaviour performed by anyone, regardless of consequences, is automatically spiritual.

The operative condition is the genuine manifestation of reflexive awareness.

But the teaching refuses to confine divine consciousness to a narrow class of solemn experiences.

And now comes something quite extraordinary.

Because this self-apprehension is connected with:

Nāda.

Sound.

And Abhinavagupta begins listing its different modes.

21. Eight forms of Nāda-Bhairava — the sound beneath sound
Now, pay attention.

The Sanskrit introduces a sequence of subtle auditory expressions.

Avyakta.

Unmanifest.

Dhvani.

Sound or resonance.

Rāva.

Roaring or reverberation.

Sphoṭa.

Bursting forth, a sudden expressive emergence.

Śruti.

That which is heard.

Nāda.

Resonance.

Nādānta.

The end or subtle limit of resonance.

And:

Anāhata.

Unstruck.

Now, the precise enumeration is compressed across the verses, but the concluding text identifies:

Aṣṭavidha Nāda-Bhairava.

The eightfold Bhairava of sound.

And here’s the important distinction.

Anāhata means unstruck.

Not produced by the ordinary collision of objects.

So we’re discussing a traditional yogic conception of subtle sound.

Not simply ordinary acoustic vibration.

And Abhinavagupta connects the sequence with:

Mantravīrya.

The potency of mantra.

Now, that’s remarkable.

Because remember Chapter Three?

The ultimate phonemic powers emerge from the divine self-apprehension of consciousness.

And audible Sanskrit is only one level of that manifestation.

Here, too, we’re moving toward the subtle source of sound.

From articulated resonance toward its unmanifest ground.

And Abhinavagupta gives several pairs or circles in which this is contemplated.

The movements of prāṇa and apāna.

Gamana and āgamana.

Going and coming.

Their points of repose.

The two ears.

The two eyes.

The relevant paired bodily supports.

And the condition in which the movements come together and cease.

Now, here’s the subtle point.

Sound isn’t being treated merely as something produced by a mouth and received by an ear.

The practitioner is being directed toward the relationship between the manifestation of sound and the conscious awareness through which it appears.

And Abhinavagupta describes a subtle sound associated with the Heart and throat.

And then:

Kṣobha-vigama.

The cessation of agitation.

Kṣobha — stirring, disturbance, excitation.

Vigama — disappearance, cessation.

At this cessation, the differentiated sounds subside.

And the supreme form of Nāda-Bhairava is apprehended.

Now, that is particularly beautiful.

Because the whole sequence concerns the emergence and withdrawal of manifest sound.

And where does it end?

Not merely in physical silence.

But in the luminous consciousness through which both sound and silence appear.

And Abhinavagupta connects this with:

Jyotis.

Light.

And:

Dhvani.

Sound.

The luminous and sonorous expressions of the same divine reality.

And he names the result:

Paramā māntrī vyāptiḥ.

The supreme pervasion of mantra.

Now, that’s an absolutely magnificent phrase.

Because the mantra’s true pervasion isn’t simply the sound travelling through the air.

It’s the recognition of its divine power throughout experience.

And here’s where Abhinavagupta delivers one of the most important claims in the entire chapter.

22. Jīvanmukti — liberation in every action
The text says:

Evaṃ karmaṇi karmaṇi viduṣaḥ syāj jīvato muktiḥ.

Now, let’s break that down.

Evaṃ — thus, in this way.

Karmaṇi karmaṇi — in each and every action.

Viduṣaḥ — of the one who knows.

Jīvataḥ — while living.

Muktiḥ — liberation.

So:

In every action, the knower may be liberated while still alive.

Now, that is magnificent.

Because we’ve spent so many chapters discussing liberation after initiation, after death, through ritual purification and through divine grace.

And now Abhinavagupta returns to:

Jīvanmukti.

जीवन्मुक्ति

Liberation while living.

Not merely the promise of a future heavenly state.

Not merely the dissolution of the body.

But liberation within embodied existence.

And the extraordinary claim is that this can be expressed:

Karmaṇi karmaṇi.

In each action.

Now, consider what that means.

Walking.

Speaking.

Listening.

Eating.

Thinking.

Laughing.

Experiencing pleasure.

Experiencing discomfort.

The entire movement of embodied life.

And within each of these, the knower recognises the conscious reality through which the experience occurs.

Now, this is the real culmination of the Kaula teaching.

Not simply that you can perform an unusual ceremony.

But that the relationship between experience and consciousness can be transformed throughout life.

And here’s the distinction.

The ordinary limited individual experiences actions as belonging to a small independent self.

“I am doing this.”

“I am experiencing that.”

“This is mine.”

“That is outside me.”

But the realised practitioner apprehends the same manifestations within the freedom of one consciousness.

Śiva acting through Śakti.

And the apparent individual no longer needs to interpret the entire field as something fundamentally alien.

Now, that’s the teaching.

But Abhinavagupta isn’t finished.

Because we’ve reached a particularly esoteric set of verses concerning the integration of fire, wind, the trident and the senses.

And one of these verses contains something rather dangerous.

So we need to distinguish its symbolic and historical meaning carefully.

23. The last esoteric seals — fire, wind, the senses and a dangerous leap
The next verses concern:

Parakula-vijñāna.

The knowledge of the supreme Kula.

And the practitioner is called:

Garbha.

A womb or receptacle of that knowledge.

Now, isn’t that a beautiful reversal?

Throughout the chapter we’ve discussed manifestation emerging from the divine womb of Śakti.

And now the knower becomes a receptacle for the higher knowledge.

The verse then speaks of the relation between:

Śūnya.

Void.

And:

Aśūnya.

Nonvoid.

And the establishment of:

Anala and anila.

Fire and wind.

Within a single axis.

Ekadaṇḍa.

Then comes:

Samarasīkaraṇa.

समरसीकरण

Making of one flavour.

Let’s unpack it.

Sama — equal, same.

Rasa — flavour, essence, relish.

Kṛ — to make.

So:

Bringing things into one homogeneous essence or flavour.

Now, that’s a magnificent word.

Because it’s not simply saying that different things happen to exist together.

It’s describing their integration into one experiential reality.

And the text applies this to:

Śūla.

The trident.

Remember the three powers?

The triadic manifestation.

The entire relationship between consciousness and its powers?

Well, the trident is brought into samarasa.

One flavour.

And Abhinavagupta gives the image:

Rase rasam iva sthitam.

Like one liquid essence resting within another of the same kind.

Now, that’s gorgeous.

Because once the distinction between two substances has vanished in a homogeneous mixture, you can’t simply point to one portion and announce:

“That’s the absolutely separate essence.”

The whole arrangement is of one nature.

And that’s the intended contemplative relation between the powers of consciousness.

Now comes:

Tyaktāśaṅka.

Having abandoned doubt.

And:

Nirācāra.

Free from the constrictions of fixed conduct.

Within this esoteric context, the practitioner is being described in relation to the transcendence of ordinary limiting distinctions.

But that isn’t a universal permission to behave recklessly or violate other people’s boundaries.

And then an extraordinary phrase appears:

Nāham asmīti bhāvayan.

Contemplating, “I am not.”

Now, this is very important.

Because it could easily be misunderstood.

Abhinavagupta hasn’t suddenly abandoned the doctrine of self-aware consciousness and decided to become a philosopher of absolute personal nonexistence.

The phrase operates within a contemplative context in which the limited identification of “I” is relinquished.

The small, contracted ego isn’t the ultimate Self.

And this is exactly what we’ve been investigating since Chapter One.

Ahaṃkāra is not the same as the supreme aham.

The ego-sense is a limited formation.

But the essential I-consciousness is the self-aware luminosity of Śiva.

So the apparent negation must be understood within the movement beyond contracted identification.

Not as a proof that consciousness doesn’t exist.

And then the text directs attention toward the divine powers within the body.

Dehasthā devatāḥ.

The deities established in the body.

Even delight and agitation are apprehended within:

Cidghana.

The compact fullness of consciousness.

Now, that’s a beautiful expression.

Cit — consciousness.

Ghana — dense, compact, solid mass.

So:

A solid mass of conscious reality.

Not a physically solid substance, of course.

The term expresses undivided fullness.

Then Abhinavagupta mentions the divine groups located in the circles of the ears, eyes, mouth and nose.

And the recognition of:

Grahītṛ.

The apprehender.

The subject of experience.

And the culminating result is associated with:

Khecarī.

The divine power moving through the expanse of consciousness.

Now, one final verse describes a dramatic and dangerous physical leap associated with Vyomacarīmudrā. Its literal wording involves throwing the body toward a distant precipice or chasm, rather than merely sitting in meditation.

That is an attested part of the chapter, but it is not a safe practice to attempt, and nothing in the source establishes that invoking a mudrā protects a person from falling injuries or death.

The relevant philosophical theme is the traditional association of vyoma, space, with unrestricted consciousness. The text also contains this hazardous physical image; we shouldn’t erase it or turn it into an actionable exercise.

And with that, Abhinavagupta closes the long metrical section:

Ity eṣa yāmalayāgaḥ.

Thus is the worship of the pair.

Now, that was quite a journey, wasn’t it?

We began with Śakti and her inseparability from the possessor of Śakti.

Then the divine pair.

The inner and outer circles.

The return of the subordinate goddesses into the central consciousness.

The peaceful and manifested conditions.

The supreme Kaula beyond both.

The threefold visarga.

The generative lotus.

The Sun, Moon and fire.

The emergence of subtle sound.

Khecarī.

The sensory powers.

And the extraordinary claim that the knower can be liberated in each and every action.

Now we return to prose.

And the fifth sacrifice.

Prāṇayāga.

24. Prāṇayāga — when the entire universe becomes the movement of life
Right.

Remember the six forms?

We’ve completed:

External worship.
Worship in Śakti.
Worship in the body.
Worship in the divine pair.
Now comes the fifth.

Prāṇayāga.

प्राणयाग

The sacrifice in vital energy.

And here’s something important.

The Sanskrit description is incredibly short.

Abhinavagupta doesn’t introduce another enormous independent breathing system.

He says that the prāṇa already contemplated as pervading the universe is strengthened through offerings.

Upodbalana.

Reinforcement, strengthening, supporting.

And what supports it?

The previously described pervasion of consciousness.

Saṃvid-vyāpti.

And offerings involving food, fragrance, incense and other ritual substances.

Now, here’s the crucial distinction.

The offerings aren’t merely being presented to a physical breath.

The vital power is being contemplated as pervaded by consciousness and encompassing the universe.

Viśvamaya-prāṇa.

Prāṇa consisting of or pervading the universe.

Now, remember Chapter Five.

Prāṇa wasn’t merely the oxygen moving through the lungs.

Within the tantric subtle-body account, it was associated with a far more extensive system of vital powers and their relation to conscious manifestation.

And here the same principle operates.

The breath and vital activity become supports for recognising the whole cosmic manifestation within consciousness.

And the offerings strengthen that orientation.

Now, imagine our disciple.

“Right, Abhinava.”

“Yes?”

“We’ve gone through the altar.”

“Yes.”

“Śakti.”

“Yes.”

“The body.”

“Yes.”

“The divine pair.”

“Indeed.”

“And now prāṇa.”

“Correct.”

“So do we need another fifty pages of instructions?”

“Not this time.”

“Thank God.”

Because the essential method has already been described.

The fifth rite extends that same nondual pervasion into vital energy.

And that prepares the sixth and highest.

Saṃvidyāga.

The sacrifice in consciousness itself.

25. Saṃvidyāga — the highest sacrifice requires no separate object
Now comes the final of the six.

Saṃvidyāga.

संविद्याग

Saṃvid — consciousness.

Yāga — worship, sacrifice, sacred offering.

So:

The sacrifice in consciousness.

And Abhinavagupta defines it with extraordinary concision.

Viśrānti-rūḍhiḥ.

The firm establishment of repose.

Viśrānti — resting, repose.

Rūḍhi — becoming established, firmly rooted.

Now, that’s magnificent.

Because after all these elaborate ceremonies, the highest sacrifice is simply the establishment of repose in consciousness.

But we need to be careful about that word.

Viśrānti doesn’t merely mean relaxation.

It doesn’t mean becoming drowsy.

Or falling asleep.

Or having a particularly pleasant afternoon.

It means resting in the essential reality of awareness.

And remember what we’ve discovered about that awareness.

It isn’t simply the absence of activity.

It’s the self-aware freedom of Śiva.

The reality beyond the division between peaceful and manifested states.

So the highest sacrifice is not an action through which you manufacture consciousness.

It’s the firm recognition of consciousness as the essential reality of the entire sacrificial process.

Now think about how extraordinary that is.

You began with an altar.

A physical support.

Something outside the body.

Then a living embodiment of Śakti.

Then your own body.

Then the divine pair.

Then vital energy.

And finally consciousness itself.

Each step became more interior.

But the point wasn’t simply moving from outside to inside.

It was the progressive overcoming of the assumption that the divine reality is restricted to a particular object or event.

And in the sixth sacrifice, there is no separate object that needs to become divine.

The essential reality is recognised directly.

Saṃvid.

And Abhinavagupta says:

Prāg eva nirūpitaḥ.

It has already been explained earlier.

Of course it has.

Because we’ve spent twenty-one chapters doing precisely that.

Remember Chapter Two?

Anupāya.

The condition beyond means.

And Chapter Four?

The power of correct contemplative understanding.

And Chapter Twelve?

The highest bathing is immersion in Bhairava.

And Chapter Thirteen?

True worship is the offering of the universe into the fullness of awareness.

All those teachings converge here.

The highest Kulayāga is the recognition that the entire universe, the ritual, the worshipper and the deity are nothing but the manifestation of one consciousness.

Now, that’s absolutely magnificent.

But the chapter isn’t quite finished.

Because there is a consequence for initiation.

And then one final surprise.

Apparently the six sacrifices aren’t the complete story.

There’s a seventh.

And Abhinavagupta saves it for the very end of the book.

26. The initiation by a glance — when the Heart is free from doubt
Right.

Suppose someone has performed one of these Kaula sacrifices.

And the result is a condition in which their Heart is purified through freedom from uncertainty.

Nirvicikitsatā-pavitrita-hṛdaya.

Now, that’s a magnificent compound.

Let’s unpack it.

Nir — without.

Vicikitsā — hesitation, uncertainty, doubt.

Pavitrita — purified.

Hṛdaya — Heart.

So:

A Heart purified through the absence of doubt.

Now, remember Chapter Twenty-One.

We were discussing:

Prasiddhi.

Firmly established conviction.

And the way a teaching becomes rooted in the Heart.

Here the same theme returns in ritual form.

The disciple has entered into a state of conviction concerning the divine reality.

And what may the guru do?

Dīkṣā.

Initiation.

But here’s the remarkable thing.

The initiation can occur through:

Avalokana.

A glance.

Now, that should sound familiar.

Remember Chapter Eleven?

We discussed the genuine guru’s ability to bestow initiation through knowledge, speech, vision and other means.

Well, here’s another example.

The guru first shows the disciple the relevant Kaula rite.

And then, through the previously described contemplative pervasion:

Vyāpti.

And:

Anusandhāna.

Sustained conscious connection or recollection.

The guru performs initiation by the glance.

Now, that’s extraordinary.

Because after all these elaborate initiatory systems, the text permits a comparatively direct operation for someone whose Heart has genuinely attained the relevant purity.

And it doesn’t depend upon the usual sequence of sesame and ghee oblations as a necessary preliminary in this case.

Now, remember:

Tila.

Sesame.

Ājya.

Clarified butter.

We encountered their oblations in the earlier initiations.

But here the method is explicitly distinguished.

The guru’s contemplative apprehension and glance become decisive.

Now, we shouldn’t present this as proof that looking at someone can reliably alter their consciousness or religious status.

It’s a traditional claim about initiation through realised divine awareness.

But the inner structure is consistent with everything we’ve learned.

The closer the practitioner is to recognition, the less elaborate the external means may need to be.

And for other rites?

Abhinavagupta says the earlier procedures still apply.

Parokṣadīkṣā.

Initiation in absence.

And the other occasional rites.

But now with the Kaula sacrifice occupying the principal position.

Etad-yāga-pradhānatā.

The predominance of this sacrifice.

So the previous ritual framework isn’t simply abolished.

It’s reorganised around the Kaula understanding.

And now comes the final sentence.

The end of Chapter Twenty-Two.

And, in the checked editions, the end of the Tantrasāra itself.

And after all the complexity, the conclusion is astonishingly simple.

27. The seventh sacrifice — the guru’s own body
Now listen carefully.

We’ve had six sacrifices.

External.

Śakti.

Body.

The pair.

Prāṇa.

Consciousness.

But Abhinavagupta now says:

Guruśarīre saptamaḥ kulayāgaḥ.

गुरुशरीरे सप्तमः कुलयागः।

The seventh Kaula sacrifice is in the body of the guru.

And what is its status?

Sarvottamaḥ.

The highest of all.

Now that’s quite an ending.

Because we might have expected the sixth sacrifice in consciousness to be the absolute conclusion.

And indeed, it’s explicitly principal for the liberation-seeker.

But the final sentence adds a seventh form centred upon the guru.

Why?

Because the guru, within this initiatory tradition, is understood as the living embodiment of the knowledge through which the divine reality is transmitted.

And Abhinavagupta says that the seventh sacrifice, performed once together with the previously described worship, fulfils everything.

Sakṛd eva kṛtaḥ sarvaṃ pūrayati.

Now, let’s break that down.

Sakṛt — once.

Eva — indeed, only.

Kṛtaḥ — performed.

Sarvam — everything.

Pūrayati — fills, completes, fulfils.

So:

Performed just once together with the earlier sacrifice, it fulfils the whole.

Now, that is a remarkable claim.

Because the guru is no longer merely one participant standing beside the sacred ritual.

Their very body becomes the support of the highest Kaula worship.

And this takes us straight back to Chapter Eighteen.

Remember what Abhinavagupta said about qualification?

Genuine knowledge is primary.

Not merely the status of guru.

Not merely consecration.

Not merely the impressive religious title.

The guru must actually possess the liberating understanding.

And within that conception, the guru’s body becomes significant as an embodiment of the supreme knowledge.

Now, that doesn’t mean worshipping an ordinary teacher’s personality as though they possessed independent divine authority.

Nor does it legitimise exploitation or unquestionable obedience.

The ritual claim presupposes the spiritual qualification described throughout the work.

And the philosophical meaning is the same one we’ve encountered from the very beginning.

The guru, the disciple, the Goddess and the entire manifestation are ultimately non-different from Parameśvara.

The guru’s body becomes a culminating ritual support for that recognition.

And then comes the final phrase.

ACTUAL ENDING OF TANTRASĀRA CHAPTER 22

गुरुशरीरे सप्तमः कुलयागः सर्वोत्तमः सोऽपि प्राग्यागसाहित्येन सकृदेव कृतः सर्वं पूरयतीति शिवम्॥
Guruśarīre saptamaḥ kulayāgaḥ sarvottamaḥ so’pi prāgyāgasāhityena sakṛd eva kṛtaḥ sarvaṃ pūrayatīti śivam.

And what are the final words?

Iti śivam.

इति शिवम्।

Thus, auspiciousness.

Thus, Śiva.

And that’s it.

There is no separate concluding metrical stanza after this sentence in the two checked witnesses. The chapter concludes in prose, followed by the authorial colophon.

Not another elaborate ritual.

Not another description of the tattvas.

Not another metaphysical argument.

Simply:

Śivam. gretil.sub.uni-goettingen.de +1

28. The end of the Tantrasāra — the whole bloody thing was Śiva
And now.

Consider what we’ve just read.

Twenty-two chapters.

We began with consciousness.

The supreme light.

Prakāśa.

And its power of self-apprehension.

Vimarśa.

We established its freedom.

Svātantrya.

Its capacity to manifest everything.

Then the apparent limitation of the individual.

The emergence of Māyā.

The tattvas.

The worlds.

The cosmic pathways.

The phonemes.

The mantras.

The different conditions of subjectivity.

And the way the one consciousness can appear as innumerable limited beings.

Then we discussed the methods of recognition.

Anupāya.

Śāmbhavopāya.

Śāktopāya.

Āṇavopāya.

And the various forms of divine immersion.

Samāveśa.

Then the descent of grace.

Śaktipāta.

The initiation of the disciple.

The purification of the cosmic pathways.

The sacred baths.

The ritual worship.

The consecration of gurus.

The rites associated with death.

The continuing conduct of initiated practitioners.

And the authority of the scriptures.

All of it.

And finally, here in Chapter Twenty-Two, we reach the Kaula sacrifice.

A progressive movement through increasingly interior forms of worship.

From the external support.

To Śakti.

To one’s own body.

To the divine pair.

To prāṇa.

And finally:

Saṃvid.

Consciousness itself.

And what was the supreme teaching?

That the divine reality isn’t confined to any particular manifestation.

Not merely the stillness.

Not merely the activity.

Not merely the bliss.

Not merely the absence of bliss.

Not merely the world.

Not merely withdrawal from the world.

But the unrestricted consciousness that is the reality of both.

Anuttara.

The unsurpassable.

And now we can finally understand why Abhinavagupta insisted upon the Kaula character of the ritual.

Because the whole point is the conscious integration of what appears differentiated.

The body.

The senses.

The vital power.

The Goddess.

The creative emission.

The universe.

The ritual participants.

And the awareness through which everything becomes manifest.

Nothing independent remains outside the ultimate reality.

And here’s the extraordinary irony.

The highest sacrifice doesn’t require sacrificing consciousness to attain something beyond it.

It’s consciousness recognising its own nature.

And the external rites become meaningful insofar as they support that recognition.

Now, think back to the beginning of the book.

Chapter One.

Remember the opening philosophical claim?

Jñānaṃ mokṣakāraṇam.

Knowledge is the cause of liberation.

And what was the ignorance that had to be overcome?

The contracted apprehension through which consciousness seemed limited.

And now the whole thing has returned to exactly that problem.

The apparent individual.

Paśu.

Believes itself separate.

The world appears other.

The deity appears distant.

The guru appears to possess some power that the disciple lacks.

The rituals appear to be complicated external mechanisms for acquiring something new.

But through the course of the teaching, Abhinavagupta has progressively undermined those assumptions.

The individual is a manifestation of Śiva.

The world is a manifestation of Śiva.

The deity is a manifestation of Śiva.

The guru’s liberating power is attributed to Śiva.

And the awareness through which all of these are apprehended?

Śiva.

So the ultimate problem wasn’t that the Supreme Lord had disappeared.

Or that consciousness lacked some foreign property.

Or that the universe had somehow escaped beyond divine freedom.

The problem was the limited apprehension of what was already present.

And the whole Tantrasāra has been a progressive clarification of that recognition.

Now, here’s the thing.

Abhinavagupta doesn’t end by saying:

“Reject the universe.”

Or:

“Reject the body.”

Or:

“Reject the senses.”

Or:

“Reject every experience.”

He concludes with a ritual tradition in which all of these become possible supports for recognition.

But neither does he conclude:

“Do whatever you like and call it Śiva.”

He has spent whole chapters distinguishing qualification, knowledge, appropriate conduct, initiation and the actual purpose of worship.

The recognition of nonduality is not the same thing as indiscriminate indulgence.

And that distinction is absolutely essential.

Because the Kaula path is not simply a theology of pleasure.

It’s a theology of conscious freedom.

Pleasure can appear.

Pain can appear.

Repose can appear.

Activity can appear.

The body can act.

The senses can move.

And all of these are expressions of the one consciousness whose nature is unrestricted.

Now, what does that mean for liberation?

Jīvanmukti.

Liberation while living.

Not merely the promise of attaining some other place after death.

But the recognition of the divine reality throughout embodied existence.

Karmaṇi karmaṇi.

In each action.

And that’s the extraordinary culmination.

Not that you have to spend your whole life performing increasingly elaborate rituals.

But that the ultimate nature of the ritual can be recognised within the very awareness through which life is experienced.

And then, after all those chapters, Abhinavagupta adds the seventh sacrifice.

The guru’s body.

The living embodiment of the transmission.

The recognition communicated through a qualified teacher.

And finally:

Iti śivam.

Thus, Śiva.

Now imagine the disciple closing the book.

Twenty-two chapters.

Thousands of Sanskrit words.

Extraordinary metaphysics.

Detailed rituals.

Cosmic hierarchies.

The most beautiful conceptions of divine consciousness.

And the disciple turns to Abhinavagupta.

“Right.”

“Yes?”

“I’ve finished.”

“Excellent.”

“So what’s the final teaching?”

And Abhinavagupta gives a little smile.

“You were never ultimately separate from the reality you’ve been studying.”

“Oh.”

“Yes.”

“So what was all this bloody business about?”

“Understanding that.”

“Twenty-two chapters?”

“Twenty-two chapters.”

“All those tattvas?”

“Yes.”

“The six cosmic pathways?”

“Yes.”

“The mantras?”

“Yes.”

“The initiations?”

“Yes.”

“The sacred baths, the tridents, the Kālīs, the festivals, the divine couple and the entire Kaula ritual?”

“All of it.”

“And the result?”

“Recognition.”

Pratyabhijñā.

The recognition of one’s own essential nature.

And here’s the final joke.

You thought you were a little individual reading an enormous book to discover the nature of Śiva.

And Abhinavagupta has spent the entire book explaining that the reality through which the reading, the understanding, the doubt and the recognition occur is itself the supreme consciousness.

So, after all this time, who’s reading?

Who’s understanding?

Who’s questioning?

And who’s being recognised?

The whole bloody business is Śiva.

Iti śivam.

End of Chapter Twenty-Two — Kulayāgaprakāśanam.

End of Abhinavagupta’s Tantrasāra.

